  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows a broad high pressure centered over Texas \npulling a plume of monsoonal moisture north through Mexico along the \nSierra Madre Occidental into the Desert Southwest along the Arizona- \nNew Mexico Border. A trough extending from the Great Basin south to \noff the Baja Coast is helping to pull that moist plume north. This \npattern will dominate the weather for eastern Utah and Western \nColorado through the rest of the week and into the weekend, but \nforecast confidence drops off by the weekend as the models diverge \non solutions with some pulling in deeper moisture from the remnants \nof Hurricane Polo while other models push Polo west into the Pacific \nkeeping the region dry. Stay tuned for updates on this as we go \nthrough the week. \n\nCurrently a shortwave low is moving north along the Nevada-Utah \nBorder supported by an overhead jet streak across Utah and Western \nColorado. This system has been generating thunderstorms across \neastern Utah through this morning which are now developing across \nWestern Colorado as the jet streak slides to the east over the \nregion. The 18Z sounding shows a fairly unstable atmosphere above \nthe cool surface layer. Look for storm coverage to increase through \nthe afternoon with diurnal heating under mostly clear to scattered \nskies. With 50 to 80 mph winds aloft, the primary threat with these \nstorms are damaging winds gusting 60 mph or more. The Storm \nPrediction Center has northeastern Utah and northwestern Colorado \nare under a marginal convective outlook for damaging winds \nassociated with the thunderstorms this afternoon and evening. Though \npwats arn't so impressive today at 0.79 inches, lightning and brief \nperiods of heavy rain are always threats with passing thunderstorms. \nSeek shelter if you are outside when These storms roll through; \"when \nthunder roars, go indoors.\" \n\nTomorrow through Friday will see an increase of showers and \nthunderstorms across southwestern Colorado as the southerly flow \npushes the moisture up on the southern face of the San Juan \nMountains. We have issued a Flood Watch starting a noon tomorrow \nthrough 6 AM Friday for zone 19, the southern face of the San Juan \nMountains, and for zone 22, the area south to the Colorado-New \nMexico Border where 1.50 inches rain or more is possible. If you are \nin these areas, you should monitor forecast updates and be alert for \npossible Flood Warnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding \nshould be prepared to take action should flooding develop.",
